task: merge-chunked-order
agent: opus5-intent
started: 2026-09-09
branch: feature/merge-chunked-order
worktree: ../okay-wt-mergeorder

why: the only known REAL defect open. Found by gate-honesty's own
  gate and filed rather than guessed at; taking it now because a
  finding nobody chases is the worst state a finding can be in — the
  same reasoning that made actor-stop-drain today's most valuable
  work.

the evidence, and why it is not one of the day's false alarms:

    okay.TestStream "merge(chunked): the same elements as merge ..."
    obtained: 1..16, 49, 50, 17..48   (source a's OWN elements)

  - nothing lost, nothing duplicated: 100 elements, both `.sorted`
    assertions passed. So not truncation.
  - 0.068 s. So not starvation, which is what kafka (237 s against
    0.084 s alone) and intent (39 s against 0.059 s) were.
  - nothing early or extra, which is what the two okay.ui failures
    were: a `Thread.sleep` used as a synchronisation point.

  A later chunk of ONE source overtook an earlier chunk of the SAME
  source. Five isolated runs of the suite passed, so it wants
  contention.

THE QUESTION IS THE CONTRACT, and it must be settled before any fix.
  `Source.merge`'s doc is long — chunk size, capacity, `flushAfter`,
  why the size is not a parameter — and says NOTHING about order
  within a source. Two outcomes, both legitimate:

  a) per-source order IS the law. Then the chunked path has a race
     and the code is wrong.
  b) it is not promised for `chunked = true`. Then the TEST asserts
     more than the contract — and the contract must then SAY SO out
     loud, because "merge" naturally reads as interleaving sources
     without reordering within one. Silently weakening the test would
     leave the next reader with the same wrong expectation.

DISQUALIFYING EVIDENCE, registered before the work:

  - no failing repro, no fix. Five isolated runs are green, so a
    green run proves nothing here. Today's method applies: when the
    CONDITIONS cannot be reproduced, reproduce the MECHANISM — for
    the ui pair that meant setting a sleep to zero, and it worked in
    a minute after a 28-spinner load harness had failed.
  - I must not decide the contract by reading my own expectation into
    it. If the design intent is (b), the merge is correct and my
    instinct is the thing that is wrong.
  - `Chunks.merge` is a DIFFERENT function the doc points at for
    already-chunked streams. Whatever I find must be attributed to
    the right one.

predict: (a) — a race, in how chunks reach the channel rather than in
  the merge's own logic, and most likely around the `capacity /
  ChunkSize` slot arithmetic the doc describes. Recorded so being
  wrong shows.
